# PART 2 — MOBILE-FIRST PERFORMANCE FOR A WIDE RANGE OF DEVICES

Mobile phones are my primary audience. I want the experience to remain attractive without forcing visitors to use powerful hardware.

The minimum target is to attempt a stable experience around 30 FPS on tested low-end devices, while allowing more capable devices to reach 45–60 FPS when possible. These are performance targets, not results to claim without actual measurements.

## A. Implement four practical rendering modes

Integrate these modes into the existing quality system rather than creating an unrelated system.

### Auto
- Select an initial quality level using available device capabilities.
- Use device memory, hardware concurrency, pointer capabilities, viewport size, and supported graphics features only as imperfect hints.
- Do not rely exclusively on user-agent detection.
- Adapt quality gradually if measured rendering performance remains poor.
- Avoid expensive benchmark scenes or long blocking initialization.
- Remember manual overrides when appropriate.

### Low
Designed for budget and older mobile phones, including devices with approximately 3–4 GB total RAM, provided their graphics hardware and browser can support the scene.

- Set the effective render pixel ratio to a maximum of approximately 1.0, allowing a lower value such as 0.75 on very weak devices.
- Disable real-time shadow maps.
- Use a small number of essential lights.
- Replace unnecessary point lights with emissive materials and inexpensive glow decals.
- Substantially reduce ambient particles, hologram particles, and decorative animations.
- Use lightweight materials for expensive environment surfaces.
- Disable expensive post-processing.
- Reduce texture resolution and anisotropic filtering where appropriate.
- Avoid unnecessary transparency overdraw.
- Keep important holograms, player movement, navigation, and interaction feedback visible.
- Preserve sufficient contrast and readability.

### Medium
- Use a render pixel ratio cap around 1.25–1.35.
- Use inexpensive lighting and restrained shadows.
- Enable more particles and detail than Low, but avoid unnecessarily expensive effects.
- Preserve the existing performance-conscious approach to holograms and shared resources.

### High
- Preserve the current desktop visual quality as closely as possible.
- Retain the existing higher-quality lighting, particles, shadows, and materials when practical.
- Do not lower desktop quality simply to make all devices use identical settings.

Use realistic fallbacks when a feature is unsupported.

## B. Runtime adaptation

Implement a conservative performance adaptation strategy:

- Measure frame-time behavior without adding significant production overhead.
- If frame time remains consistently too high for several seconds during active gameplay, reduce graphics quality gradually.
- Use hysteresis and cooldown periods to prevent quality levels from oscillating constantly.
- Do not reduce quality based on one temporary frame spike.
- When appropriate, restore higher quality only after sustained good performance and a reasonable cooldown.
- Give visitors a manual Auto / Low / Medium / High override through the existing pause menu or settings interface.
- Persist manual preferences using the existing preference system or localStorage.
- Do not let rendering quality change player movement speed, physics, collision behavior, interaction distances, or input responsiveness.

If WebGL 2 or the required rendering capabilities are unavailable, or the device cannot maintain a usable experience, activate a lightweight alternative renderer or accessible non-3D portfolio interface.

The fallback must retain access to every portfolio section and use the same real data sources and links.

## C. Render-loop and resource management

- Never continuously render a fully active background world behind a modal that does not need it.
- Pause or throttle decorative animation on the title screen, when the document is hidden, and when inspection or feedback modals are open.
- Pause gameplay appropriately when the pause menu is open.
- Restore rendering and input correctly when a modal closes.
- Keep camera and player controls responsive during active gameplay.
- Reuse shared geometries, materials, and textures.
- Use instancing or batching for repeated decorative geometry when appropriate.
- Avoid per-frame allocations and unnecessary React state updates.
- Properly dispose of resources when they are no longer used.
- Preserve WebGL context-loss handling and recovery if already implemented.
- Do not eagerly load the models, images, and textures for every future portfolio section.

Do not regress the previous optimization work.

