# PART 5 — EVERY SECTION GETS ITS OWN ORGANIZED WORLD

This is a critical part of the design.

The main world has the six main section billboards. When the visitor selects a section, they must enter a distinct, organized area within the portfolio experience.

The section areas should feel like different rooms or locations inside the same futuristic world rather than separate, unrelated web pages.

## Shared world architecture

Prefer one existing WebGL renderer and one main canvas.

Reuse the current environment, camera, lighting system, resources, and player where appropriate.

Represent each section as a managed zone, with only the required content and active objects enabled for the current zone.

Use the existing architecture where possible. Do not create six independent full-scale worlds or six continuously rendering canvases.

A zone can reposition the player through a smooth, brief transition or activate another area of the existing environment. Choose the approach that best fits the codebase and keeps memory and rendering costs low.

When transitioning:

- Use a smooth but short transition.
- Avoid unnecessary loading screens when content is already available.
- Prevent the player from becoming stuck outside the destination zone.
- Preserve a clear return path.
- Restore camera and movement state correctly.

## A. Projects Zone layout

Support any reasonable number of projects without manually positioning each one.

Examples:

- Two projects: show them in a balanced, spacious arrangement.
- Five projects: arrange them in a visually balanced layout, with adequate distance between boards.
- Ten or more projects: arrange them in multiple rows or logical groups.

Implement a deterministic layout function that computes positions from the available items, zone boundaries, spacing requirements, and visual arrangement.

Requirements:

- Keep billboards from overlapping.
- Keep enough space for the player to move between them.
- Face labels and interactive surfaces toward the visitor when appropriate.
- Ensure that nearby boards can be distinguished and selected.
- Avoid creating excessively large, expensive, detailed environments as the project count grows.
- Load or enable only the objects required for the currently visible or nearby content.
- Provide sensible behavior for empty and loading states.

Do not hardcode a separate scene for two, five, or ten projects. One reusable layout system should support variable counts.

## B. Skills Zone layout

Organize skills into category boards based on real data.

Each category can contain individual skill items presented through compact HTML panels or lightweight 3D labels, as appropriate.

Support additional skill categories without requiring manual scene redesign.

## C. Certificates Zone layout

Arrange available certificate boards using the same reusable layout principles.

Use lightweight placeholders while certificate images are loading, and show image previews only when useful.

## D. Contact Zone layout

Arrange the available contact and social boards clearly, with readable platform names and concise guidance about each action.

Do not generate a board for a missing URL.

## E. Feedback Zone layout

The Feedback Zone needs:

- One primary feedback and review display.
- Three review cards per page or view.
- Previous and next controls.
- A clearly accessible feedback form.
- Clear submission feedback.

Do not create one full 3D object for every historical review. Keep the number of expensive 3D elements small and render review content inside a reusable HTML interface.

## F. Central Home / Return Billboard

Every section zone must contain an unmistakable central or otherwise prominent Home / Return to Main World billboard.

The billboard must work through the same approach-and-interact system as the other billboards.

Additionally, provide a compact persistent home/back control in the normal UI, especially on mobile. Visitors should never have to wander around looking for a way home.

Return navigation must restore the main-world state without unnecessarily reloading the application or fetching the entire world again.

